Jack can play with 1 quarter for 20 minutes, so his playing rate is \( \frac{1}{20} \) quarters per minute.

Two friends are significantly worse and can play half as long as Jack, so each has a rate of \( \frac{1}{20} \times \frac{1}{2} = \frac{1}{10} \) quarters per minute.

One friend is significantly better and can play 1.5 times as long as Jack, so their rate is \( \frac{1}{20} \times 1.5 = \frac{1.5}{20} = \frac{3}{40} \) quarters per minute, or equivalently \( \frac{1}{30} \) quarters per minute since \( \frac{3}{40} = \frac{1}{30} \).

The total playing rate for all four individuals (Jack and three friends) is:
\[
\frac{1}{20} + \frac{1}{10} + \frac{1}{10} + \frac{1}{30}
\]

Convert to a common denominator of 60:
\[
\frac{1}{20} = \frac{3}{60}, \quad \frac{1}{10} = \frac{6}{60}, \quad \frac{1}{30} = \frac{2}{60}
\]
\[
\frac{3}{60} + \frac{6}{60} + \frac{6}{60} + \frac{2}{60} = \frac{3 + 6 + 6 + 2}{60} = \frac{17}{60} \text{ quarters per minute}
\]

They play for 4 hours, which is \( 4 \times 60 = 240 \) minutes.

The total quarters used is:
\[
\frac{17}{60} \times 240 = 17 \times \frac{240}{60} = 17 \times 4 = 68
\]

Thus, 68 quarters were used, meaning $68 worth of money was used for the arcade game.

\boxed{68}
