Maria went out to the park with an initial charge of \(P\) percent of the power bank.

For the first 5 hours, the power bank loses 9% of its capacity each hour, so it retains 91% of its charge per hour. After 5 hours, the remaining charge is \(P \times (0.91)^5\).

For the next 3 hours, it loses 7% of its capacity each hour, so it retains 93% of its charge per hour. After 3 hours, the charge is \(P \times (0.91)^5 \times (0.93)^3\).

This final charge is given as 28%, so:
\[
P \times (0.91)^5 \times (0.93)^3 = 28
\]

Solving for \(P\):
\[
P = \frac{28}{(0.91)^5 \times (0.93)^3}
\]

Calculating the denominator:
- \((0.91)^5 \approx 0.624032\)
- \((0.93)^3 \approx 0.804357\)
- Product: \(0.624032 \times 0.804357 \approx 0.50194\)

Thus:
\[
P \approx \frac{28}{0.50194} \approx 55.78
\]

Rounding to the nearest whole number, the initial charge was approximately 56%.

Verification with \(P = 56\):
- After 5 hours: \(56 \times (0.91)^5 \approx 56 \times 0.624032 = 34.945792\)
- After 3 hours: \(34.945792 \times (0.93)^3 \approx 34.945792 \times 0.804357 \approx 28.11\%\)

The result (28.11%) is close to 28%, with a small error of about 0.11%, which is reasonable given the context.

Therefore, the charge of the power bank when Maria went out to the park was 56%.

