Question: Jason works as a salesperson at a car dealership.  He needs to sell 15 cars this month to earn a big bonus.  He knows based on historical averages, that for every 25 telephone calls he makes to potential customers, he gets one person to come into the car dealership to look at new cars.  And for every two customers that come into the car dealership, one will buy a car.  Based on these average numbers, how many telephone calls would Jason need to make to sell 15 cars and earn his bonus?
Model Response: I need to figure out how many telephone calls Jason needs to make to sell 15 cars. Let me break this down step by step.

First, he knows that for every 25 telephone calls, he gets one person who comes in to look at cars. So, the number of people who come in depends on the calls. Let \( c \) be the number of telephone calls he makes. Then, the number of people who come in is \( \frac{c}{25} \).

Next, for every two customers who come in, one buys a car. So, if there are \( p \) customers who come in, then the number of cars sold is \( \frac{p}{2} \).

He needs to sell 15 cars, so:

\[ \frac{p}{2} = 15 \]

Solving for \( p \):

\[ p = 15 \times 2 = 30 \]

